# Authored screen copy, separated into single-idea prose blocks.
copy_by_step={
'022.s01':('Resources and operations','The path identifies the resource; the method supplies the operation. A collection has no item identifier, while an item path includes one.||GET retrieves, POST performs collection-specific creation processing, PUT replaces the target and DELETE removes it.||A creation contract can pair 201 with a Location identifying the created resource. An absent item can use 404.'),
'022.s02':('Keep identity stable','Use /orders for the collection and /orders/{order_id} for an item. GET and PUT address the same item but request retrieval and replacement respectively. POST on the collection can express creation processing; DELETE on the item expresses removal.||For creation, the contract can pair 201 with Location /orders/42; for an absent item, it can use 404. The path alone never completes the operation definition: keep the method alongside it.'),
'022.s05':('Register method and path','Create a FastAPI instance, then use @app.get, @app.post, @app.put or @app.delete with the intended path to register its handler. Method plus path defines the operation, so GET and PUT on one path remain distinct.||Routes are evaluated in declaration order. Put a fixed route before a conflicting parameterized route so the latter does not capture it.'),
'022.s06':('Trace the first match','A request to /orders/current can match /orders/{order_id} if that conflicting declaration comes first. A later fixed declaration does not automatically win. Move the fixed route first.||Separately, GET and PUT on the same item path are different operations. Review method, path and declaration order rather than relying only on how specific the path looks.'),
'022.s07':('Two different distinctions','Use @app.get for retrieval and @app.put for replacement on the item path. Matching path text does not merge these operations: their methods differ.||For /orders/current and /orders/{order_id}, declaration order matters. Declare the fixed GET first, then the conflicting parameterized GET. Trace the registered method-and-path operation and the first matching route; do not assume a fixed route is always preferred.'),
'024.s01':('Validate configuration at construction','External configuration needs validation. Subclass pydantic_settings.BaseSettings and declare typed fields. Construct Settings() to read environment variables and validate them; missing required settings or invalid values fail validation.||For dotenv input, set model_config = SettingsConfigDict(env_file=".env"). Environment variables take precedence over dotenv values.'),
'024.s02':('Separate source from validity','Check construction with a valid environment setting, a missing required setting and a value incompatible with its declared type. Missing or invalid configuration fails validation instead of becoming unchecked input.||With dotenv enabled, compare conflicting values for the same key. The environment wins, but source priority does not remove type validation. Identify both the supplying source and whether its value satisfies the declaration.'),
'024.s03':('Follow the settings boundary','Declare the required typed app_name field on Settings, configure the dotenv file, and construct Settings(). This reads and validates external input.||The registered source cases separate environment-only, conflicting-source and absent-from-both conditions. An environment value wins a conflict with dotenv. If a required value is absent, construction fails; an incompatible value also fails validation. A dotenv file is not an override for the environment.'),
'025.s01':('Own the asynchronous client','Use httpx.AsyncClient and await its get, post or other request method for an asynchronous outbound call.||Reuse one scoped client for repeated calls rather than constructing clients inside a hot loop, benefiting from connection pooling. Close it at the owning scope’s end with async with or await client.aclose().'),
'025.s02':('Calls and cleanup are separate','Several requests can share one connection-owning client. Reusing that client avoids hot-loop construction and benefits from connection pooling.||Awaiting get or post performs a request; it does not prove the client has closed. Finish using the client, then end ownership through its async context manager or await client.aclose().'),
'025.s03':('Trace ownership through both lookups','For the registered shipping lookups, create one AsyncClient in an owning scope and await both request calls through it. Reuse supports connection pooling rather than making a fresh client for every call.||The scope closes the client after its requests finish, using async with or await client.aclose(). Keep the lifetime trace separate from individual request completion: an awaited request is not cleanup.'),
'026.s01':('Validate the relationship','Individually valid fields can still form an invalid booking. Use @model_validator(mode="after") on an instance method to inspect multiple already-validated fields. Raise ValueError when the invariant fails; return self on success.||For one validated field, use an after-field validator with a classmethod and return the accepted value.||Test the relationship with individually valid values that violate it: model construction must raise ValidationError. Also test that a consistent pair is accepted.'),
'026.s02':('Isolate the booking invariant','The exercise requires end to be later than start. An after-model validator checks their already-validated values, raises ValueError for an inconsistent pair and returns self for a consistent pair. Construction reports the invariant failure as ValidationError.||A single-field after-validator instead uses a classmethod and returns the accepted field value. Do not demonstrate a cross-field rule with an input that already fails its field type; that tests a different boundary.'),
'026.s07':('Reject deliberately','An absent order is an expected rejection, not a successful result containing an exception object. Raise HTTPException(status_code=..., detail=...) to stop normal operation work and produce the intended HTTP rejection.||FastAPI’s default rejection body places the supplied value under the JSON key detail. Supply headers on the exception when response metadata is required, such as an authentication challenge.||Review the status, detail structure and required headers. Raising marks the control-flow boundary; returning the exception object does not express the same instruction.'),
'026.s08':('Read the raise boundary','When the order is absent under the contract, raise HTTPException with the intended status and client-facing detail. The raise stops normal work and produces the rejection; returning the object is not equivalent.||The default body uses the key detail. If the rejection needs metadata, pass headers to the exception. Keep the detail client-facing rather than exposing internal diagnostics.'),
'028.s01':('Choose the I/O execution boundary','For awaitable I/O, use async def and await. For a blocking I/O library, use a normal def handler; FastAPI runs that path operation in an external thread pool.||An async def path operation runs asynchronously. An ordinary helper called directly from it is not automatically moved to the thread pool. A blocking call there can block the event loop.'),
'028.s02':('Follow the call into the helper','The registered shipping client performs blocking I/O. A normal def path operation places that work in FastAPI’s external thread pool. An awaitable library would instead suit async def with awaited operations.||Moving blocking I/O into an ordinary utility does not change its execution boundary. Called directly from async def, that utility is not automatically offloaded and can block the event loop. Trace the call, not just the handler’s spelling.'),
'029.s01':('Register a feature, then include it','Create an APIRouter and register the feature’s operations on it. The main application imports that router and calls app.include_router(router) to assemble its operations.||An inclusion prefix adds to local paths. Inclusion can also supply tags, dependencies and responses.'),
'029.s02':('Find the assembled path','The orders router declares local /orders. Including it with prefix /api assembles the operation at /api/orders. Tags, dependencies and responses can also be supplied at the include point.||A feature module’s presence is not application assembly. Trace registration on the router and then app.include_router: defining a router alone does not include its operations.'),
'030.s01':('Bind the URL declarations','A placeholder {order_id} plus order_id: int binds and converts the path value. Ordinary scalar parameters absent from the path, such as limit, bind from the query.||A query default permits omission; without a default, the query input is required. Its type controls supplied-string conversion.||A path value that cannot convert to its declared type produces a validation error, rather than invoking the handler with the unconverted value.'),
'030.s02':('Test one binding decision at a time','Identify the path placeholder first: it binds the order identifier. The scalar limit outside the path binds from the query. Compare omission with and without a default to determine requiredness.||Then compare convertible and nonconvertible identifiers. Invalid path conversion stops at validation; it does not deliver the original string to the handler. Location, conversion and omission are separate decisions.'),
'030.s03':('Names are not enough','For /orders/{order_id}, declare order_id: int to bind and convert the path value. A scalar limit outside that path binds from the query; a default allows omission, while no default makes it required.||Compare a convertible path string with a nonconvertible one. The latter produces a validation error, not a handler invocation with an arbitrary string. A parameter name alone does not make it a path input: the name must appear in the route path.'),
'030.s08':('Declare the public output','Set response_model=PublicOrder on the decorator to validate, serialize and filter ordinary returned values to that model. Define only the public fields so private fields are not returned merely because the internal object has them.||response_model takes priority over a return annotation for FastAPI response processing. Returning a Response directly bypasses automatic conversion and filtering; the application is responsible for its content.'),
'030.s09':('Inspect schema and return form','First compare PublicOrder’s field list with the internal order. Leaving private_processing_note out of the public model excludes it from ordinary response-model processing, which validates, serializes and filters.||Then inspect what the handler returns. A directly returned Response bypasses that automatic path. Having a public model alone does not show that a direct Response’s content was filtered.'),
'030.s10':('Trace the output boundary','PublicOrder contains only intended public fields. Configure it with response_model=PublicOrder; ordinary response processing validates, serializes and filters to that model rather than including every internal field.||If a return annotation is also present, response_model takes priority. A directly returned Response instead bypasses automatic model conversion and filtering, leaving its content to the application. Check the return form before assuming the private field was excluded.'),
'032.s01':('Declare the supplied input','Use Annotated[Type, Depends(provider)] to declare an input that FastAPI supplies by executing the provider. Pass the callable provider, not provider(). FastAPI can then resolve the provider’s inputs.||Dependencies expose needed inputs at the handler and let multiple operations reuse input-provision logic rather than relying on an invisible global lookup.'),
'032.s02':('Read from consumer to supplier','The handler declares a needed type and names its supplying callable through Depends. FastAPI resolves that callable’s inputs, executes it and gives the returned value to the handler.||Pass provider rather than calling provider() in the declaration. Another operation can declare the same reusable provider. This makes the supplier visible; it does not establish an application-lifetime value store.'),
'032.s03':('Callable, resolution, value','Annotated[Type, Depends(provider)] describes input provisioning: resolve the provider’s inputs, execute it and supply its result to the handler. Passing the callable preserves that instruction; calling it in advance is different.||Multiple operations can declare the same input supplier. Keep each needed input explicit at its consumer rather than hiding it in a global lookup.'),
'033.s01':('Distinguish wait limits from elapsed time','HTTPX has connect, read, write and pool timeouts. Read and write limits bound waits for individual chunks, not whole-request elapsed time. Configure a finite client or request timeout; timeout=None disables enforcement.||Exceeded waits raise corresponding HTTPX exceptions: ConnectTimeout, ReadTimeout, WriteTimeout or PoolTimeout.||For a required total elapsed budget, use async with asyncio.timeout(seconds). Its cancellation becomes TimeoutError outside the context.'),
'033.s02':('Identify which boundary expired','A finite HTTPX timeout bounds specific I/O waits. Successive response chunks may each arrive within the read limit while total elapsed time exceeds that limit; the read timeout is not a whole-request deadline. timeout=None disables enforcement.||When the contract requires a total asynchronous-work budget, wrap the operation in asyncio.timeout. Catch its TimeoutError outside the context. Distinguish that total-budget boundary from HTTPX’s connect, read, write and pool timeout exceptions.'),
'034.s01':('Separate responsibilities, not just files','A dependency can extract reusable input provisioning and expose it as a declared handler parameter.||Keep HTTP adaptation in transport handlers, application rules in business callables and stored-state access in persistence adapters. Each responsibility should be able to change without embedding the others.||Move the order decision to a plain callable with explicit inputs, leaving HTTP adaptation in the handler and storage access in an adapter. Splitting the same mixture across files is not enough.'),
'034.s02':('Trace the order rule by role','The handler adapts HTTP inputs and outputs. A plain callable with explicit inputs makes the business decision; a persistence adapter accesses stored state. A dependency can supply reusable inputs through a declared parameter.||Separate those responsibilities so each can change without embedding the others. Count roles, not files: module boundaries alone do not establish separation of concerns.'),
'034.s05':('Schedule bounded follow-up work','Declare a BackgroundTasks handler parameter and register work with background_tasks.add_task(callable, *args, **kwargs). It runs after the response is sent.||The callable may be def or async def; FastAPI handles either. Work remains in the application process, so process failure can prevent its survival or delivery.'),
'034.s06':('Response delivery is not task completion','The registered order follow-up is small, optional and process-local. Declare BackgroundTasks, register the callable with add_task and then return the response. The task runs after response sending; it can be def or async def.||A process failure after the response can lose pending work. Scheduling does not prove survival, delivery or durable completion. Use this boundary only for work whose contract fits that limitation.'),
'034.s10':('Resolve prerequisites before consumers','A provider can declare Annotated[Type, Depends(other_provider)] for its own input, forming a dependency graph. FastAPI resolves those prerequisites before calling the provider and supplies its result to the consuming operation.||A sub-dependency can declare typed query inputs. These are extracted from the request before its result reaches the dependent provider.'),
'034.s11':('Follow pagination toward the operation','The pagination provider declares typed page and page_size query inputs. FastAPI extracts them, resolves pagination and passes its result to order-options before calling order-options. The operation then receives that provider’s result.||Trace the graph from request inputs to prerequisite providers to consumers. A provider does not run first and obtain unresolved prerequisites afterward.'),
'037.s01':('Middleware is a wrapper stack','Configure maintained middleware through app.add_middleware(MiddlewareClass, option=value), using documented options rather than implementing its protocol.||The last addition is outermost: it sees requests first and responses last. With A added first and B second, requests traverse B → A → handler; responses traverse A → B.'),
'037.s02':('Trace each direction separately','Record A as the first addition and B as the second. The handler sits inside A, which sits inside B. Requests enter through B then A; responses leave through A then B.||Configure each maintained component through app.add_middleware with its documented options. A directionless “middleware order” list hides the reversal: the last addition sees requests first and responses last.'),
'037.s05':('Serve the application without reload','Start the supplied application with uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000. In the import string, main is the module and app is its application object; verify both against the project.||--reload is useful in development but should not be enabled in production: it consumes resources and is less stable.||--workers starts multiple worker processes and is mutually exclusive with --reload. Choose the worker count for the deployment; reload is not production process management.')}

def lin(s):
 return {k:copy.deepcopy(s.get(k,[])) for k in ['concept_ids','teaching_block_ids','fact_ids','segment_ids','anchors']}
def prose(text,lineage,role='definition'):
 return {'type':'prose','text':text,'role':role,'lineage':copy.deepcopy(lineage)}
def tb_for_facts(c,fs):
 return [c+'#'+b['id'] for b in bs[c]['teaching_blocks'] if set(b['fact_ids'])&set(f.split('#')[1] for f in fs)]
def code_block(code,notes,language='python'):
 lines=code.splitlines(); annotations=[]
 for needle,text in notes:
  i=next(i for i,l in enumerate(lines,1) if needle in l)
  annotations.append({'start_line':i,'end_line':i,'text':text})
 return {'type':'code_walkthrough','language':language,'code':code,'annotations':annotations,'reveal':'all'}
def graph(labels,edges,alt,kind='mapping',flow=False):
 nodes=[{'id':k,'label':v} for k,v in labels.items()]
 b={'type':'process_flow' if flow else 'diagram','nodes':nodes,'edges':[dict(zip(['from','to','label'],e)) for e in edges],'alt_text':alt}
 if flow:b['direction']='LR'
 else:b['diagram_kind']=kind
 return b

